                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n+1
          %p
            %strong Homotopies.
            Once a diagram has been constructed, we can modify it by applying a
            = succeed "," do
              %em homotopy
          %p
            %strong Renderer (experimental).
            Diagrams can be rendered in both 2d and 3d. At the moment, only the 2d renderer is interactive, allowing diagrams to be manipulated directly using the mouse; the 3d renderer is for display only, and is currently quite buggy, only working reliably for small diagrams with a connected geometry. It is hoped that
            %em homotopy.io
            will soon allow rendering and smooth animation of arbitrary 3d composites.
          %p
            %strong Projection.
            Diagrams can be high-dimensional, and hence hard to display on a computer screen.
            %em Projection
            causes lower dimensions of the diagram to be ignored; for example, given a 5-dimensional diagram with projection set to 3, you will see a single
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 5
                    %mo −
                    %mn 3
                    %mo =
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (5-3=2)
          %p
            %strong Slice.
            For an
            = succeed "-diagram" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \gt 2
            %em Slicing
            allows an
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            %strong Undo and redo.
            Use the browser back and forward buttons to undo and redo your interactions with the proof assistant.
          %p
            %strong Server.
            The proof assistant has a backend that lets you log in and save proofs to the server. You can also make proofs shareable, and obtain a short URL suitable for sharing with others, or embedding in a research paper. In the future you will also be able to publish proofs, obtaining a permanent date-stamped URL.
          %h2#homotopies Homotopies
          %p A composite diagram can be deformed by clicking and dragging in its interior. If the indicated deformation would be geometrically invalid, an error message is displayed in the lower-right corner, and the diagram does not change. Otherwise, a new diagram is displayed, which is homotopic to the original diagram.
          %p
            Conjecturally, homotopies can be used to construct arbitrary progressive deformations of the
            = succeed "-diagram" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
          %p Of course, the proof assistant manipulates finite combinatorial structures, and we use the word ‘homotopic’ with reference to their presumed geometrical realization. We do not make this precise here, nor has it yet been made precise in the literature; for this reason, our use of the term ‘homotopy’ is not yet well connected to the standard notion.
          %p
            Every homotopy is either a
            = succeed "," do
              %em contraction
            = succeed "," do
              %em expansion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            as a zigzag (see
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#CombinatorialFoundation"} Combinatorial foundation
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D \to D'
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo ′
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D' \to D
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D'
          %h3#base_cases Base cases
          %p
            A homotopy is triggered by choosing an
            = succeed "-diagram" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 2
            = succeed ";" do
              %em vertically
          %p
            If the chosen vertex is in
            = succeed "," do
              %em generic position
            %a{:href => "#CombinatorialFoundation"} Combinatorial Foundation
            section.)
          %p
            To illustrate this, we suppose we have the following diagram, where for convenience we label the vertices with numbers (
            %a{:href => "https://homotopy.io/?id=QMw1sG1p4yCpsgL3Xh7K"}>
              %i live workspace
            ):
          %p
            %img{:src => "https://www.cs.bham.ac.uk/~vicaryjo/homotopy.io/nlab/contraction-expansion-labelled.png", :width => "250"}/
          %p Vertices 1 and 4 are in generic position, so dragging them will trigger contractions, while dragging vertices 2 and 3, which are in singular position, would trigger expansions. We illustrate the resulting diagrams as follows:
          :css
            table&gt;tr&gt;td { border:none!important }
          %table{:style => "border-style:hidden !important; padding:0px!important"}
            %tr
              %td
                %img{:src => "https://www.cs.bham.ac.uk/~vicaryjo/homotopy.io/nlab/2-up.png", :width => "200"}/
              %td
                %img{:src => "https://www.cs.bham.ac.uk/~vicaryjo/homotopy.io/nlab/2-down.png", :width => "200"}/
              %td
                %img{:src => "https://www.cs.bham.ac.uk/~vicaryjo/homotopy.io/nlab/4-down.png", :width => "200"}/
              %td
                %img{:src => "https://www.cs.bham.ac.uk/~vicaryjo/homotopy.io/nlab/1-up.png", :width => "200"}/
            %tr
              %td
                %i Expansion
              %td
                %i Expansion
              %td
                %i Contraction
              %td
                %i Contraction
            %tr
              %td
                %i 2 up or 3 down
              %td
                %i 2 down or 3 up
              %td
                %i 1 down
              %td
                %i 4 up
          %p Dragging vertex 1 up, or vertex 4 down, has no effect, as vertices cannot be dragged off the top or bottom edge of a diagram.
          %p
            The contractions illustrated above are unique. However, in some cases, contractions are non-unique, as illustrated here (
            %a{:href => "https://homotopy.io/?id=9DDbCH0V7xdKULkqroIz"}>
              %i live workspace
            ):
          %table{:style => "border-style:hidden !important; padding:0px!important"}
            %tr
              %td
                %img{:height => "88", :src => "https://www.cs.bham.ac.uk/~vicaryjo/homotopy.io/nlab/unitcounit-2.png"}/
              %td →
              %td
                %img{:height => "133", :src => "https://www.cs.bham.ac.uk/~vicaryjo/homotopy.io/nlab/unitcounit-1.png"}/
              %td ←
              %td
                %img{:height => "88", :src => "https://www.cs.bham.ac.uk/~vicaryjo/homotopy.io/nlab/unitcounit-3.png"}/
          %p Starting with the central diagram, we produce the leftmost diagram by dragging the upper vertex south-east or the lower vertex north-west, and produce the rightmost diagram by dragging the upper vertex south-west or the lower vertex north-east.
          %p Contraction is not always possible. The following images illustrate non-contractible scenarios:
          %table{:style => "border-style:hidden !important; padding:5px!important"}
            %tr
              %td
                %img{:height => "133", :src => "https://www.cs.bham.ac.uk/~vicaryjo/homotopy.io/nlab/noncontractible-1.png"}/
              %td
                %img{:height => "133", :src => "https://www.cs.bham.ac.uk/~vicaryjo/homotopy.io/nlab/noncontractible-2.png"}/
            %tr
              %td
                (
                %a{:href => "https://homotopy.io/?id=HWcBiyNFuYg3dGrlOqZh"}>
                  %i live workspace
                )
              %td
                (
                %a{:href => "https://homotopy.io/?id=2JlXs4ei0lGsUGeHWJtr"}>
                  %i live workspace
                )
          %p In both of these cases, attempting to perform the contraction will give an error message. In the first case, the contraction fails because there is no canonical ordering on the rear wires; this is analogous to the nonunique contraction case given above, but here, because of the projection, we cannot give extra data to break the symmetry. In the second case, the contraction is successful at the level of the untyped diagram, but it is rejected by the type checker, since it merges two distinct generating cells, changing their neighbourhoods in a way which is not allowed by the type system.
          %p If there are no slice controls displayed, then the homotopy is being performed at codimension 0 (that is, at the top level), and the diagram will be rewritten according to the homotopy. If every slice control shows S or T, then we are viewing a boundary of the main diagram, and the homotopy we are building will be attached to that boundary. Otherwise, we are in the interior of the diagram, working in some positive codimension, and the homotopy must now be propagated to lower codimension, using the recursive scheme detailed in the next section.
          %h3#recursive_cases Recursive cases
          %p When we apply a homotopy to an interior slice of the main diagram, it is propagated up to the top level. A homotopy can be either a contraction or an expansion, and we can be in a regular or singular subslice; this gives 4 cases, which we consider separately.
          %h2#CombinatorialFoundation Combinatorial foundation
          %p The combinatorial data stored by the proof assistant can be understood in terms of zigzags.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              In a category
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %em zigzag
              is a finite diagram of the following sort:
            %p
              %img{:alt => "A zigzag", :height => "120", :src => "https://www.cs.bham.ac.uk/~vicaryjo/homotopy.io/nlab/zigzag.png", :style => "margin-left:auto; margin-right:auto;"}/
            %p
              We write
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Z
                      %mi r
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi r
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi r
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z_r = \{ r_0, \ldots, r_n \}
              for the set of
              = succeed "," do
                %em regular slices
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Z
                      %mi s
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z_s = \{ s_0, \ldots, s_{n-1} \}
              for the set of
              = succeed "." do
                %em singular slices
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              Given zigzags
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Z
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z,Z'
              in a category
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %em zigzag map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi Z
                    %mo →
                    %mi Z
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:Z \to Z'
              comprises a monotone function
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mtext s
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi Z
                        %mtext s
                      %mo →
                      %mi Z
                      %msub
                        %mo ′
                        %mtext s
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_\text{s} : Z_\text{s} \to Z'_\text{s}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi Z
                      %mtext s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \in Z_\text{s}
              a morphism
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mi i
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi s
                        %mi i
                      %mo →
                      %mi s
                      %msub
                        %mo ′
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi f
                            %mtext s
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi i
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_i : s_i \to s' _{f_\text{s}(i)}
            %ul
              %li
                Take the disjoint union of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Z
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z'
                as diagrams in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %li
                Add the arrows
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_i
                between singular objects.
              %li In between these arrows, add equalities between regular objects.
            %p The idea is illustrated in the following picture:
            %p
              %img{:alt => "A zigzag", :height => "200", :src => "https://www.cs.bham.ac.uk/~vicaryjo/homotopy.io/nlab/zigzagmap.png", :style => "margin-left:auto; margin-right:auto;"}/
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              Given a category
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %em zigzag category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Z
                      %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z_C
              has zigzags as objects, and zigzag maps as morphisms.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              An
              %em
                untyped
                = succeed "-diagram" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              is an object of the category obtained by starting with the terminal category
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              times.
          %p
            For a signature
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |\Sigma|
            for its set of generating types, which is equipped with a dimension function
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mtext dim
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{dim}:|\Sigma| \to \mathbb{N}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}_\Sigma
            for the poset with objects given by elements of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |\Sigma|
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo →
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \to y
            just when
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mtext dim
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mtext dim
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{dim}(x) \lt \text{dim}(y)
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_5 Definition
            %p
              A
              %em
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |\Sigma|
                \-typed
                = succeed "-diagram" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              is an object of the category obtained by starting with the category
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒫
                        %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}_\Sigma
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              times.
          %h2#type_checking_and_normalization Type checking and normalization
          %p
            Given a
            = succeed "-typed" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |\Sigma|
            = succeed "-diagram" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %em well-typed
            with respect to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+n-category"} associative n-category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Globular"} Globular
          %div
            %p
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof+assistants"}> proof assistants
                \:
            %p
              based on plain
              = succeed "/" do
